title Season G thirteenth rung — Ed25519 signing in pure Rye, whole scheme
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Rye-first crypto-parity wave; approve your own design rounds and make progress before panicking to check in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop only at custody gates. baton prin recur.
think With scalar multiply-add mod L GREEN and sent (twelfth rung, this same session), every arithmetic piece Ed25519 signing needs stood GREEN — SHA-512, scalar reduction mod L, the scalar ladder, point compression, and now the multiply-add. The design doc names this plainly: once a Rye signing primitive is parity-GREEN, Lotus's signed carry becomes buildable — the library is engineering; only signing with the maintainer's own identity key is the custody gate. Signing over TEST seeds a selftest generates is non-gated: a test seed is not the maintainer's key.
think Composed the RFC 8032 §5.1.6 procedure exactly, writing no new mathematics: expand the seed through SHA-512, CLAMP the low half into the secret scalar a (clear low three bits for the cofactor, clear bit 255, set bit 254 for range), take the high half as prefix; publish A = compress([a]B); derive r = SHA-512(prefix ‖ M) mod L; form R = compress([r]B); derive k = SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) mod L; set S = (r + k·a) mod L via the GREEN mul_add(k, a, r). mul_add handles a unreduced (it multiplies full-precision then reduces), so the clamped a needs no pre-reduction. Bounded both hash buffers (prefix ‖ M and R ‖ A ‖ M) by a named max, refusing MessageTooLong — the same posture verify carries.
think Parity target: Zig's std.crypto.sign.Ed25519 as an independent RFC 8032 implementation. The RFC §7.1 secret-key vectors are not in the tree and I would not risk a mistyped secret literal, so I proved parity through Zig byte-for-byte on BOTH the derived public key and the signature across a spread, which is genuinely independent — Zig itself conforms to RFC 8032. Strengthened it with a round-trip through my own GREEN verifier (import ed25519_verify.rye; no cycle, verify never imports sign), a determinism check (same seed and message → identical bytes), and the oversize-message refusal.
obs GREEN on the first metal pass. Across 8 deterministic seeds × 5 messages (40 signatures), our derived public key equals Zig's byte-for-byte, our signature equals Zig's byte-for-byte, every signature verifies under our own GREEN verify, a one-byte tamper of each is refused, signing is deterministic, and a message one byte past the bound is refused with the named error. The Ed25519 scheme now stands whole in pure Rye — sign and verify, both parity-GREEN, no std.crypto and no C library.
obs Fixed a first-draft tautological assert in derive_public (an is_canonical-or-not that is always true) into two real invariants: derivation is a pure function of the seed (full byte-equality on re-derivation), and the key is never the identity encoding. tame_style bans clean; the new file lands in none of the fail lists. Honest scope held: signs only TEST seeds, no real key, no network, no funds, no real device — no custody gate crossed. A constant-time signing path over the secret scalar is the Season's named timing horizon, wanting measurement not a claim.
loom witness=crypto_ed25519_sign_witness seeds=8 messages=5 signatures=40 pubkey_parity=byte_exact sig_parity=byte_exact roundtrip=GREEN result=GREEN
